== fuller page ==
One word can carry several meanings; C4 keeps them all. Corwin gave three
of its words their meanings on 2026-08-03, and they explain each other
[three].

A seam is "an opportunity to better pin someone's language": not a defect,
not a gap to fill, but an opening, where someone gets to tell us what they
meant [seam].

A pin is "a statement someone has made, about anything." Every word starts
as "an arrow in flight", and "needs to land somewhere to become a pin
(noun)" [pin]. So Pin is a mouse, a pin holds a note to the wall, and a pin
is what someone said.

A semaphore, the last name on the S door, is how we unite terms around data,
and data becomes information as they unite. As a verb, it routes pins toward
places they can land, "by ripping seams open to find openings for users to
provide nuance, correct our bias, and make the meaning personal" [semaphore].

The rule underneath: a name with several senses is one name for several
truths, and must not be "resolved". The real hazard is two copies of one
truth that can quietly drift apart, a cache and its file; those need a
named tiebreaker. "Multiplicity of sense: keep. Duplication of fact: fence."
[keep]

C4's own name is a seam. Its README calls it Collaborative Consent
Continuity Controls [readme]; the public page on spread one said Continuous
Creation, Command & Control. A note from 2026-08-19 says both were running
then [both].

