== narration ==
# Take A, "the company talks to you": every voice speaks to the listener.
[picture A]
narrator: Here is a big wooden wheel. Can you find the spoke that's a different colour?
narrator: A spoke is part of a wheel.
spoke: And I'm Spoke. I'm a friend, and I see things.
narrator: Now you try. Say hi.
spoke: You did it! You said hi, so you spoke.
pin: One word, three things, and all of them true. I'm writing them all down for you.
[fuller A]
pin [three]: Corwin gave three words their meanings, all on one day. Here they are, for you.
spoke [seam]: A seam is an opening. It's the place where someone gets to tell us what they really meant.
pin [pin]: A pin is a statement someone has made. A word starts out as an arrow in flight, and it has to land somewhere to become a pin.
spoke [semaphore]: Semaphore is the last name on my door. It's how words come together around what we know. And it rips seams open, so you can tell us what you mean.
narrator [keep]: Here is the rule underneath. One word with many meanings: keep it open. Two copies of one fact: put a fence round them.
pin [readme]: Even the name {C4|C four} is a seam. Its own front page says Collaborative Consent Continuity Controls.
spoke [both]: And the door said something else. A note from August says both were running. You can keep them both too.

# Take B, "the lap reader": one close voice reads to you; Spoke and Pin
# mostly talk to each other, and turn to you only in a short aside.
[picture B]
narrator: A spoke is part of a wheel.
narrator: Spoke is a friend who sees.
spoke: Pin. Pin! Someone just said hi.
pin: Then they spoke, Spoke.
spoke: That's my name.
pin: One word, three things. All true. I'm writing them all down.
[fuller B]
narrator [three]: One word can carry several meanings, and {C4|C four} keeps them all. Corwin gave three of its words their meanings in August.
narrator [seam]: A seam is an opportunity to better pin someone's language. Not a defect. An opening.
pin [pin]: That's me, the noun. A pin is a statement someone has made, about anything.
spoke [pin]: And a word in flight is still an arrow, until it lands.
narrator [semaphore]: A semaphore unites terms around data. As a verb, it rips seams open, so people can add nuance and correct our bias.
narrator [keep]: Multiplicity of sense: keep. Duplication of fact: fence.
spoke [readme]: Pin, the front page says Collaborative Consent Continuity Controls.
pin [both]: And the door said Continuous Creation. I've kept both. They aren't fighting.

